WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.540
Allocation sizes often come from element
counts, byte counts, string lengths,

2
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.876
record counts, structure sizes, headers,
and metadata fields.

3
00:00:07.956 --> 00:00:12.177
Those values may be combined through
multiplication and addition before memory is

4
00:00:12.257 --> 00:00:12.860
allocated.

5
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.448
If the combined size is wrong,

6
00:00:14.528 --> 00:00:18.809
every later operation that trusts the
allocation size is working from a bad premise.

7
00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:22.989
Multiplication and addition used for
allocation must be checked before calling

8
00:00:23.069 --> 00:00:24.275
allocation functions.

9
00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:27.009
Element count times element size, header
plus body,

10
00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:29.923
length plus terminator, structure plus
trailing data,

11
00:00:30.003 --> 00:00:34.224
and alignment adjustments are all common
places where a result can exceed the range

12
00:00:34.304 --> 00:00:35.570
of the destination type.

13
00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.992
A too-small allocation followed by
initialization, parsing, copying, formatting,

14
00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:42.363
or appending can create memory-safety
risk.

15
00:00:42.443 --> 00:00:45.880
The allocation call may succeed because
the computed size is small,

16
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.699
while later code behaves as if the
original larger request was satisfied.

17
00:00:49.779 --> 00:00:54.060
The defensive goal is to reject or handle
unsafe numeric values before allocation or

18
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:54.441
copy.

19
00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:58.562
Use sizeof in a way that follows the
actual object or pointed-to type rather than

20
00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:00.149
hardcoded byte assumptions.

21
00:01:00.229 --> 00:01:04.510
Expressions such as element count times
the size of the pointed-to object are easier

22
00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:08.691
to review than expressions that repeat a
type name or constant in several places.

23
00:01:08.771 --> 00:01:12.871
The code should make clear which object
shape the allocation is meant to support.

24
00:01:12.951 --> 00:01:14.640
C strings need terminator space.

25
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.036
If a string length comes from parsing,
measurement, or metadata,

26
00:01:18.116 --> 00:01:22.337
allocation should account for the content
and the terminating null character when

27
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:24.166
the result is meant to be a C string.

28
00:01:24.246 --> 00:01:28.346
The same idea applies to any format that
needs sentinel storage, header storage,

29
00:01:28.426 --> 00:01:29.572
or alignment padding.

30
00:01:29.652 --> 00:01:33.210
Safe allocation math is a policy decision
as well as a technical check.

31
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.908
When a value exceeds a safe limit, the
program might reject the request,

32
00:01:36.988 --> 00:01:41.209
return an error, stream the data, chunk
the work, or use a different bounded design.

33
00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:44.002
What it should not do is convert,
multiply, allocate,

34
00:01:44.082 --> 00:01:47.700
and then trust a result whose
representability was never established.
